The "problem" with Nethack patches is that these are patches for the  
source files, not for the executable.
This is also a good thing because that means that the same patch can be  
used for different versions of nethack (Windows, Mac, Linux, Pocket PC,  
...) and you don't need a different patch for each executable.
Nethack being pretty old code, everything (almost) is hardcoded into the  
executable and you cannot "mod" Nethack by simply editing a couple of text  
files (compared to more moderns games such as Wesnoth or even commercial  
ones such as Space Empire IV or Europa Universalis 2 where lot of data is  
stored in text files and can be edited at will, thus providing an easy way  
to mod the things).

Anyway, back to Nethack. The patch is, indeed, C code as it is a piece of  
source code to be inserted at the right place in the Nethack code  
(actually, it's a bit more than simply C code since it also contains the  
indications as to where the changes should apply, that the meaning of the  
'+' and '-' at the beginning of some lines).

The other problem is that Nethack, still due to its pretty old base code  
and the high number of different systems available can be a real challenge  
to compile...

So, if you really really want the patch, here is what you have to do:
1/ Get the Nethack source code from the official Nethack site.
2/ Get some C compiler for your system.
3/ Compile Nethack. Read carrefully the instructions explaining how to do  
it. Do what they say *precisely*. Don't try anything fancy at this point.
3bis/ In case of troubles, you can come back here and ask for help. There  
are usually people able to give you advices.
4/ Once you manage to have your own running version of Nethack (be sure to  
install it in a different directory than the one you're usuing currently  
or you might think that things are OK while they're not), you can start  
trying to patch.
5/ You need a program called "patch" to apply the patch. Well, at least  
that's the name on unix-based system. I don't know how it's called on  
Windows, but I guess something like that should exist.
6/ Use "patch" to actually apply the patch to the source code (you may  
want to have a fresh copy of it first in order to keep the official code  
in a safe place as backup).
7/ Compile (again) the patched version of the code. You should now have a  
working patched copy of Nethack.


I really insist on first compiling an upatched version of Nethack. There  
are often many small problems that appear during compilation and sometimes  
new problems appear due to the patch. Better solve the problems one by  
one...

